{"claims":[{"excerpt":"# Two Emissions Tracks in the Acid Rain Program","claim":"The Acid Rain Program had two distinct pollutant-specific tracks.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The sulfur dioxide mechanism applies an aggregate cap and allows trading; one allowance covers one ton. Its phase I began in 1995 with 263 initial units at 110 plants; substitution or compensating units brought the phase I total to 445 units. Phase II began in 2000. The final 2010 cap was 8.95 million tons. The nitrogen oxides component was rate-based for certain coal-fired units, began in 1996 and did not introduce a cap or allowance trading.","narrative":"The Acid Rain Program addressed sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector, but it did not use one shared emissions mechanism.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source describes different mechanisms and timelines for sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides.","materiality":"Frames the piece’s central comparison."},{"excerpt":"Under Title IV, the Acid Rain Program addressed sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector through different mechanisms.","claim":"Under Title IV, the Acid Rain Program addressed sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector, using different mechanisms for the two pollutants.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"EPA’s Acid Rain Program addresses sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector under Title IV. The sulfur dioxide mechanism applies an aggregate cap and allows trading; one allowance covers one ton. ... The nitrogen oxides component was rate-based for certain coal-fired units, began in 1996 and did not introduce a cap or allowance trading.","narrative":"The Acid Rain Program addressed sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector, but it did not use one shared emissions mechanism.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source identifies both pollutants, t